REGIMES RUN: ['unseen_chemotype', 'unseen_ligand', 'unseen_series', 'unseen_conditions']
replicate reproducibility over 313 replicated cells: median within-cell sd 0.237 (pooled 0.776; 36 cells with sd > 1 are probably not true repeats). Implied MAE floor vs a single new measurement ≈ 0.19 (median) / 0.62 (pooled).

READING THIS REPORT
  * macro_mae weights each ECFP cluster equally (one ligand = one vote); pooled_mae is
    row-weighted; the largest ECFP cluster (the macro voting unit) is 41% of rows and the largest single extractant 30%. They can disagree — the macro is primary.
  * fold grouping differs by regime: unseen_chemotype -> tanimoto_cluster; unseen_ligand -> ecfp_cluster; unseen_series -> series_id; unseen_conditions -> condition_id.
    Ligand regimes group on the cluster, not the extractant: 91 extractants collapse to 74 bit-identical ECFP clusters, and grouping on the extractant would put
    identical fingerprints on both sides of a fold.
  * within_lig_r2 is DEPLOYABLE (raw error vs within-ligand variance; a constant-per-ligand predictor
    scores < 0). within_lig_r2_shape centres both sides on their per-ligand means and grants a free
    offset — it scores the response *shape* only. Read the two together. BOTH are row-weighted,
    even more so than pooled_mae: the largest ligand holds ~41% of the within-ligand
    variance, so neither has a macro (equal-ligand) counterpart. Do not rank arms on them alone.
  * *_SHUF arms are the same arm with its own feature block permuted. An arm that does not beat
    its shuffled twin is not using that block.
  * NULL_metal_mean / NULL_global_mean use no ligand information at all. NULL_extractant_mean and
    NULL_nearest_condition need the ligand in training: under a ligand-held-out regime they have
    nothing to fall back on and collapse to NULL_global_mean (they are printed for completeness,
    not as three separate controls).

